WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:06.650
Not every sentient anomaly the SCP Foundation
contains is a monstrosity hell-bent on destroying

00:00:06.650 --> 00:00:10.330
the world, or a dangerous apex predator with
a taste for D-Class.

00:00:10.330 --> 00:00:13.130
In fact, most aren’t.

00:00:13.130 --> 00:00:16.820
Most sentient anomalies are perfectly docile,
even polite!

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:21.839
They range from helpful talking animals to
self-aware AI to former Foundation personnel

00:00:21.839 --> 00:00:22.839
themselves.

00:00:22.839 --> 00:00:26.869
There’s little need for these low-security
anomalies to be treated with the same paranoid

00:00:26.869 --> 00:00:34.100
caution reserved for anomalies like SCP-939
and SCP-049, and for some of them the Foundation

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:36.170
can even help them out a little bit.

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:38.420
One of these anomalies is SCP-4149.

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:46.030
At a cursory glance, SCP-4149 seems to be
some sort of standard anomalous location,

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:49.110
not at all uncommon for the Foundation to
deal with.

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:51.910
Just stick a lock on it and call it a day,
right?

00:00:51.910 --> 00:00:54.679
But the containment procedures tell a different
story.

00:00:54.679 --> 00:00:59.739
MTF personnel are ordered to act like they
are visitors of an art gallery when inside

00:00:59.739 --> 00:01:02.170
SCP-4149.

00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:07.390
Not just that, but any “character breaks”
have to be played off like live theater.

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:12.390
What kind of strange anomaly requires hardened
agents and researchers to act like performance

00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:13.390
artists?

00:01:13.390 --> 00:01:17.600
Let’s go back to when the Foundation first
discovered SCP-4149.

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:24.100
It was 2007 in Chicago, and Foundation agents
across the city were staking out art galleries,

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:27.840
painting exhibitions, museums, and other fine
art repositories.

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:34.910
It wasn’t some wild goose chase to find
a painting-based anomaly like SCP-1155 or

00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:41.220
SCP-3864, but instead a concerted operation
to root out anomalies and people associated

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:44.770
with the notorious Group of Interest: Are
We Cool Yet?

00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:47.450
Don’t let Are We Cool Yet?’s name fool
you.

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:52.280
The Foundation has a long and storied history
with this group of subversive, nonconformist

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:55.200
artists that sprang up after World War 2.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:02:00.160
They’ve changed over the years in political
attitudes and direction, but one thing remains

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:03.390
consistent: they make anomalous art.

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:08.300
And not just anomalous art - dangerously anomalous
art.

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:13.230
Paintings that drive people to madness, sculptures
that kill the viewers (sound familiar?), and

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:16.730
installations that result in dozens of casualties.

00:02:16.730 --> 00:02:21.730
These anomalous art-terrorists are extremely
dangerous, and no one knows that better than

00:02:21.730 --> 00:02:22.730
the Foundation.

00:02:22.730 --> 00:02:26.790
Chicago, being known for its art, is one of
their favorite haunts.

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:31.300
So when the Foundation launched a stakeout
operation tracking known Are We Cool Yet?

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:34.590
members, they knew they’d find something
eventually.

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:39.610
That happened in June, when two Foundation
agents tailing a group of Are We Cool Yet?

00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:43.500
Members saw them moving a number of paintings
out of a truck and into what seemed to be

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:45.280
a regular condominium.

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:49.180
The agents, excited to finally get a lead,
jumped out of the car and quietly followed

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:52.040
after them, right into the door of the building.

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:55.880
They rushed in with their guns to find…
nothing.

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:59.640
But on the second floor, things were more
interesting.

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:03.640
One of the Are We Cool Yet members was laying
on the ground, writhing in pain and covering

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:04.870
his face.

00:03:04.870 --> 00:03:09.730
Upon being poked by one of the agents, he
lowered his hands, revealing a gory sight.

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:12.700
The man’s face was warped like a Picasso
painting.

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:17.640
His nose was on his forehead, his mouth was
slanted all the way to the left of his face

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:20.340
and his eyes were on his tongue.

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:24.690
He cries that the gallery did this to him,
and that his friend ran in.

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:26.940
The agents turn into the bedroom.

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:31.600
Instead of a wall and window, a large hallway
leading into a space that doesn’t exist

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:36.090
outside the building is present: SCP-4149.

00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:40.470
The last things the agents see before calling
for backup: another Are We Cool Yet member

00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:45.230
shaking hands with what appears to be a gigantic
sloth in a three-piece suit.

00:03:45.230 --> 00:03:52.300
By the time MTF arrived with special anti-cognitohazard
gear, they were all ready to enter SCP-4149.

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:57.230
The four man team enters, rounding a corner
into what appears to be a makeshift art gallery

00:03:57.230 --> 00:04:02.910
filled with anthropomorphic animals - lizards
in loungewear, birds in fancy dresses, and

00:04:02.910 --> 00:04:05.950
the large sloth in a suit the agents had seen.

00:04:05.950 --> 00:04:10.140
Along the walls are paintings and artworks
blurred by the agent's special eyewear.

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:16.170
It doesn’t take long before the team are
noticed by the animals, SCP-4149 instances,

00:04:16.170 --> 00:04:21.009
who start criticizing their acting - in English,
to the MTF’s surprise.

00:04:21.009 --> 00:04:25.199
The heavily-armed men start hearing complaints
and incompetent social commentary about their

00:04:25.199 --> 00:04:28.960
lack of subtlety from a growing crowd of clothed
giant animals.

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:33.360
Confused, they’re eventually rescued by
the sloth, who plays off their strange arrival

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:38.740
as a performance piece gone awry and dismisses
the crowd to other art pieces.

00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:44.090
After very slowly dragging them into a corner,
the besuited sloth politely introduces himself

00:04:44.090 --> 00:04:48.570
as Professor Xorkanoff, the owner of this
art gallery.

00:04:48.570 --> 00:04:53.749
He claims to be a “man” of culture, an
enjoyer of fine art who found he could only

00:04:53.749 --> 00:04:58.580
truly operate his gallery how he wanted free
of the bounds of any one world.

00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:04.029
In fact, the entirety of SCP-4149 exists in
its own pocket dimension.

00:05:04.029 --> 00:05:08.529
Upon being asked, he explains that the Are
We Cool Yet member was trying to sell him

00:05:08.529 --> 00:05:12.330
some work, but it wasn’t refined enough
for the Professor’s tastes.

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:16.960
But the artist seems to have run off to another
planet to train, specifically one lacking

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:17.999
an atmosphere.

00:05:17.999 --> 00:05:25.360
The Professor agrees to be designated as SCP-4149-2
and undergo interviewing to help the Foundation

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:30.349
understand what the situation surrounding
his gallery is, which is where the Foundation

00:05:30.349 --> 00:05:34.600
finds most of their information for the anomaly’s
description.

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:39.919
SCP-4149 is what’s known as an extradimensional
space - a location that doesn’t quite exist

00:05:39.919 --> 00:05:43.729
in any one reality, but by itself in a pocket
dimension.

00:05:43.729 --> 00:05:47.780
It’s accessible through the hallway in the
apartment building, which the Foundation has

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:50.409
bought and slated for demolition.

00:05:50.409 --> 00:05:57.199
Inside SCP-4149, one can access a large warehouse
serving as a gallery, containing upwards of

00:05:57.199 --> 00:06:02.639
70 different art pieces, ranging from fancy
oil paintings to contemporary sculptures and

00:06:02.639 --> 00:06:03.870
modern art.

00:06:03.870 --> 00:06:09.360
Almost all of them are cognitohazardous in
one way or another - extremely dangerous to

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:11.259
the untrained human mind.

00:06:11.259 --> 00:06:16.699
The gallery itself seems to be populated by
a wide variety of entities, not just the finely-dressed

00:06:16.699 --> 00:06:19.139
animals that caught the MTF off guard.

00:06:19.139 --> 00:06:25.430
They claim to come from all the various neighboring
universes and realities that SCP-4149 borders,

00:06:25.430 --> 00:06:28.259
and seem to be fine looking at all of the
art pieces.

00:06:28.259 --> 00:06:35.740
And of course, the proprietor of the establishment:
SCP-4149-2, Professor Xorkanoff.

00:06:35.740 --> 00:06:39.729
Two meters tall, dressed in a nice waistcoat
and tweed jacket.

00:06:39.729 --> 00:06:45.529
Foundation agents all describe him as friendly
and polite to a fault - even if he is a giant

00:06:45.529 --> 00:06:46.529
sloth.

00:06:46.529 --> 00:06:52.139
Unfortunately, that means everything he says
or does is at a snail’s pace - or more accurately,

00:06:52.139 --> 00:06:53.339
a sloth’s pace.

00:06:53.339 --> 00:06:59.180
But he usually spends his time plodding between
the various art pieces and explaining or discussing

00:06:59.180 --> 00:07:02.330
them with the guests as curator of the gallery.

00:07:02.330 --> 00:07:06.789
Where the Professor gets the artworks isn’t
quite clear, nor is how.

00:07:06.789 --> 00:07:12.339
He says he can travel through universes and
worlds to some degree, but this is unconfirmed

00:07:12.339 --> 00:07:18.669
– for now, all the Foundation knows is that
sometimes the works in SCP-4149 just suddenly

00:07:18.669 --> 00:07:21.330
seem to cycle out with new attractions.

00:07:21.330 --> 00:07:25.110
The gallery isn’t a perfect socializing
space, though.

00:07:25.110 --> 00:07:30.499
The entry that exists in the apartment building
is a design flaw, a construction error - but

00:07:30.499 --> 00:07:35.969
not one that can be easily fixed without tearing
the entire place down and starting again,

00:07:35.969 --> 00:07:42.399
something the Professor has sounded very upset
at, when someone deciphered his glacial yelling.

00:07:42.399 --> 00:07:46.419
SCP-4149 is friendly to Foundation interests,
though.

00:07:46.419 --> 00:07:50.490
The Professor doesn’t want uninvited guests
to know about his gallery any more than the

00:07:50.490 --> 00:07:51.909
Foundation does.

00:07:51.909 --> 00:07:57.870
To that end, he’s cut a deal: so long as
SCP-4149-2 submits to background interviews

00:07:57.870 --> 00:08:05.830
and SCP-4149 is carefully kept under containment,
SCP-4149-2 will be permitted to keep operating

00:08:05.830 --> 00:08:10.429
his gallery and the Foundation will protect
it against hostile intruders.

00:08:10.429 --> 00:08:11.839
What intruders, you ask?

00:08:11.839 --> 00:08:14.900
Well, you’ll have to look to the interviews
for that.

00:08:14.900 --> 00:08:18.569
The main interview is conducted by a D-class,
through an earpiece.

00:08:18.569 --> 00:08:22.710
The giant sloth’s statements have to be
sped up multiple times to be understandable

00:08:22.710 --> 00:08:24.050
on the recording.

00:08:24.050 --> 00:08:29.080
It starts off simple - Xorkanoff is the best
human approximation to the Professor’s real

00:08:29.080 --> 00:08:30.080
name.

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:35.110
Him coming from another world, presumably
one inhabited by other sloths, his name wasn’t

00:08:35.110 --> 00:08:37.620
designed to be spoken by simple humans.

00:08:37.620 --> 00:08:44.089
But he explains: the universe that SCP-4149
exists in is a shell, a universe that has

00:08:44.089 --> 00:08:49.290
undergone heat death and become completely
empty before being compacted into just enough

00:08:49.290 --> 00:08:52.149
space to build SCP-4149.

00:08:52.149 --> 00:08:57.650
The Professor, having some ability to pop
from universe to universe, decided to make

00:08:57.650 --> 00:09:01.900
this place his home and host to his love of
art and expression.

00:09:01.900 --> 00:09:07.399
He seems reluctant to talk about his original
home, though, until directly asked by the

00:09:07.399 --> 00:09:08.540
D-class.

00:09:08.540 --> 00:09:09.840
Then he explains.

00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:15.090
He was a man in well-standing in his home
universe, on a world inhabited by creatures

00:09:15.090 --> 00:09:16.780
similar to himself.

00:09:16.780 --> 00:09:23.800
He enjoyed all the fruits of society: culture,
music, social occurrences - and yes, art.

00:09:23.800 --> 00:09:27.920
Unfortunately, the rest of his society didn’t
share his views on art.

00:09:27.920 --> 00:09:34.190
They considered it not only a waste of time
- but an insult, an offensive level of arrogance.

00:09:34.190 --> 00:09:38.800
Being a highly technological society, they
thought art and humanities were inappropriate

00:09:38.800 --> 00:09:40.620
expressions of emotion.

00:09:40.620 --> 00:09:45.650
But from the Professor’s point of view,
all this had done was create a deeply unhappy,

00:09:45.650 --> 00:09:47.779
deeply uneducated society.

00:09:47.779 --> 00:09:52.060
His private art collection in his manor was
a closely-guarded secret.

00:09:52.060 --> 00:09:54.699
But eventually, the secret got out.

00:09:54.699 --> 00:09:59.960
His name was spat out as a cultural extremist,
a subversive nonconformist who was giving

00:09:59.960 --> 00:10:02.680
dangerous ideas to the next generation.

00:10:02.680 --> 00:10:08.320
A mob formed, and did what mobs do best: they
chased him from his home at night and burned

00:10:08.320 --> 00:10:09.430
his collection.

00:10:09.430 --> 00:10:13.949
Barely escaping with his life and the few
pieces of art he could grab, the Professor

00:10:13.949 --> 00:10:17.180
swore to rebuild his collection for the joy
of all.

00:10:17.180 --> 00:10:22.449
As the interview continues, the D-class reveals
he is blind - that’s how he’s avoided

00:10:22.449 --> 00:10:26.540
being driven insane by any of the many cognitohazardous
art pieces.

00:10:26.540 --> 00:10:30.200
The Professor hasn’t heard of blindness
before, but sympathizes.

00:10:30.200 --> 00:10:33.079
Eventually, things settle into a familiar
rhythm.

00:10:33.079 --> 00:10:38.020
Professor Xorkanoff continues to operate his
gallery for the pleasure of all his visitors,

00:10:38.020 --> 00:10:43.149
and the Foundation need only station one armed
guard in the apartment instead of spending

00:10:43.149 --> 00:10:47.770
time, money, and effort forcing everyone into
inconvenient containment in the middle of

00:10:47.770 --> 00:10:49.129
a crowded city.

00:10:49.129 --> 00:10:54.589
But just a few short months later, the Foundation
receives a call from the agent on duty, requesting

00:10:54.589 --> 00:10:55.629
backup.

00:10:55.629 --> 00:11:00.430
Once again, the MTF arrived on the scene,
kitted out for cognitohazards.

00:11:00.430 --> 00:11:04.800
They enter into SCP-4149, heavily armed and
armored.

00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:09.870
This time, they find something much more surprising
and dangerous than an art exhibition.

00:11:09.870 --> 00:11:14.170
The guests are lurking around the edges of
the walls, but the Professor is in the middle

00:11:14.170 --> 00:11:18.940
of the floor, arguing extremely slowly with
two more giant sloths.

00:11:18.940 --> 00:11:23.600
They’re dressed in tactical combat gear
and holding what looks to be guns.

00:11:23.600 --> 00:11:28.870
Their yelling, sped up, is made clear: the
newcomers are berating the Professor for expressing

00:11:28.870 --> 00:11:32.620
his art and for running from something called
the ‘Xenarthra Council’.

00:11:32.620 --> 00:11:37.360
When they finally notice the presence of the
MTF, they scoff and gradually raise their

00:11:37.360 --> 00:11:41.699
weapons, telling the MTF to leave while they
still have the chance.

00:11:41.699 --> 00:11:45.690
But while one of them is busy yelling at the
sloths to drop their weapons, another one

00:11:45.690 --> 00:11:50.149
of the troops simply walks over and pulls
the guns out of the clawed paws of the sloths:

00:11:50.149 --> 00:11:52.260
“They’re sloths, man.”

00:11:52.260 --> 00:11:56.790
By the time they slowly realize their weapons
are no longer in their hands, the sloths panic

00:11:56.790 --> 00:12:01.600
at the thought of these strange furless creatures
moving so many times faster than they ever

00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:03.100
thought possible.

00:12:03.100 --> 00:12:07.459
Just before they’re handcuffed, the pair
disappear in a burst of green light - and

00:12:07.459 --> 00:12:11.209
at this, all the guests burst into applause.

00:12:11.209 --> 00:12:17.540
This conflict, this emotion, this humour:
this is the performance they were waiting

00:12:17.540 --> 00:12:19.710
for the whole time!

00:12:19.710 --> 00:12:26.620
Now Check out Killer Living Graffiti SCP-1155
- Predatory Street Art or SCP-683 - Refrigerator

00:12:26.620 --> 00:12:30.319
Art for more art-related anomalies that will
blow your mind!

